{"items":[{"recordId":"teach-n0001076","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"For an abstraction λx.M assigned level ℓ, the body M stays at level ℓ rather than moving to a new level.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"If the variable x must be shared, its replicator Rₓ is assigned level ℓ + 1, one level above the abstraction's body.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0001077","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The translated term has an initial level assigned to it before any traversal of its syntax begins.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"That initial level is 0, which is the outermost level of the whole translated term.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0001078","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"A bound occurrence is connected to a wire endpoint whose level is determined by traversing the source syntax to that occurrence.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The endpoint therefore receives the level reached at the occurrence's position during that syntax walk.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0001079","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"When traversal enters a lambda body, it keeps the current level unchanged.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"When traversal follows an application's function, the level also stays unchanged; entering the application's argument instead increases the level by one.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0001080","units":[{"role":"definition","basis":"source","text":"For a replicator port, the connected occurrence has an occurrence level and the replicator has its own replicator level.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"derivation","basis":"source","text":"The port delta is computed as the connected occurrence level minus the replicator level.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0001081","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"In λx. x (g x), the first x occurrence has level 0, while the x occurring inside the application argument has level 2.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The sharing replicator Rₓ has level 1, giving its two port deltas as ⟨−1, +1⟩.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0001082","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The linear term (λx.x)(λy.y) reduces by one fan annihilation step.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"The result of that reduction is λy.y.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0001083","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"In λx.x x, the bound variable x occurs twice in the body, so its single bound input has two consumers.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"Because one input must feed two consumers, the translation uses a two-port replicator.","requires":[],"citationRequired":true}]}]}
